From 601606d2f556b7074d1ca96dffbf4b7a5ac3a88c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sasha Levin <sashal@kernel.org>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 10:39:30 +0100
Subject: mm/mglru: don't use stack pointers when walking PUD and PMD tables

From: Josef Bacik <josef@toxicpanda.com>

[ Upstream commit 65a9bf08a3e4d6e58df211ed900c16bafb79b267 ]

MGLRU passes stack copies of page-table entries in walk_pud_range() and
walk_pmd_range() when walking to the next level. These copies can become
stale when khugepaged or MADV_DONTNEED (CONFIG_PT_RECLAIM) removes a PTE
table concurrently.

This pattern dates back to commit bd74fdaea146 ("mm: multi-gen LRU:
support page table walks"). Likely, earlier locking masked the latent
bug. Later changes exposed it: commit 1d65b771bc08 ("mm/khugepaged:
retract_page_tables() without mmap or vma lock") allowed concurrent
retraction, and commit cf4a65539c13 ("memcg-v1: no need for memcg locking
for MGLRU") dropped the walker's outer RCU protection.

The fix is simple, so just fix the original commit so we don't have to
speculate. To fix it, pass the actual PMD/PUD pointers. This restores the
RCU-protected lookup and validation against the live PMD. Note that the PUD
handling is required as well to handle folded page tables correctly in all
cases.

Fix generated by an LLM and sent by Josef Bacik in private.

[ david: handle walk_pud_range() as well, clarify+simplify description,
         adjust Fixes: ]

Fixes: bd74fdaea146 ("mm: multi-gen LRU: support page table walks")
